write('review-lens-register.md',`# Multidisciplinary review coverage\n\nAll listed lenses are applicable to this broad platform audit. This table records one coordinated review, not independent professional approvals or multiple human reviewers. Where runtime, qualified review or operations evidence was unavailable, the conclusion is explicitly limited.\n\n| Lens | Conclusion | Findings / evidence |\n|---|---|---|\n${lensRows.map(r=>'| '+r.join(' | ')+' |').join('\n')}\n\nManufacturing/BOM, multi-entity consolidation and predictive employee scoring were not treated as mandatory product requirements: the repository/current customer brief does not establish them. Legacy production tables do not justify reintroducing a manufacturing module. See optional BI scope for the product decision.`);

write('executive-assessment.md',`# Stoquify platform correctness assessment\n\n**Verdict: substantial operational foundation, but not yet demonstrated as a complete, reliable offline and professional accounting/payroll platform.** The schema can represent most requested business facts. The important gaps are preserving those facts through currency conversion, corrections, offline recovery and complete close, plus qualified country/provider and deployed-operation evidence.\n\nAudit: 30 September 2026. Baseline commit: 62a56036a5ac9b37236a20c9b148052199069479. The initial working tree already had 129 modified and 1,240 untracked entries; findings concern that captured working tree, not the commit alone. Application/schema source was preserved; only this audit directory was authored. See final source-fingerprint verification in ${report('evidence-index.md')}.\n\n## What the evidence supports today\n\nStoquify has meaningful source-owned services for online POS, stock movements and valuation, purchasing/AP, payment evidence, reconciliation, HRIS and payroll. Transactions, idempotency, locks, approval separation, evidence hashes, immutable payroll snapshots and read-model provenance are implemented in many critical paths.\n\nThe **strongest demonstrated local evidence** is for online POS/stock/accounting-source infrastructure and tenant-scoped service behavior. All 541 stock-level quantities agree with recorded physical movements; sampled reserved/available/value arithmetic and tenant joins passed. The commerce and platform unit cohorts passed. These results support continued controlled development/piloting, not an unconditional production release declaration.\n\nThe inferred primary customer is a Cameroon/OHADA-context small or medium business operating sales, stock, cash, purchasing and people across locations, with owner/manager, cashier, inventory, procurement, finance/accountant, HR/payroll and employee personas. This inference comes from current services, role/module contracts, EN/FR surfaces and the CM country registry. A validated segment-specific requirements/acceptance dossier was not supplied. Organization/location support must not be conflated with legal-entity consolidation, and schema currency fields do not establish correct multi-currency accounting.\n\n## What prevents a complete-platform claim\n\n1. **Accounting correctness:** foreign-currency entries can store incorrect base amounts; reports can combine nominal currencies and still report a balanced result (F01). Locked accounting currency remains mutable (F07).\n2. **Correction consistency:** generic reversal can reverse a POS journal without the corresponding business transaction (F02).\n3. **Offline sales:** concurrent enqueue can lose a sale, in-flight events can become stranded after restart, and replay is deliberately test-authorized only (F03–F05).\n4. **Professional close:** a period can become CLOSED despite a broader assurance blocker; general-ledger opening balance and partial fiscal-year generation also need correction (F06, F08, F09).\n5. **Statutory and external readiness:** Cameroon CNPS is draft, IRPP and e-invoicing require expert review; live payment/authority acceptance and deployed operations were not proved (F11).\n6. **BI completeness:** operational reports have useful provenance, but complete connected financial metrics, cash-flow reporting and several decision-support capabilities remain partial (F12).\n\n## Evidence strength\n\n| Evidence | Result | Practical limit |\n|---|---|---|\n| Schema inventory | 211 models; all local model tables/column names present | Full type/index/privilege drift and production metadata not certified. |\n| Migration history | 87/87 local checksums match applied migrations | Fresh replay and populated upgrade were not performed. |\n| Unit/service/component/action tests | **${totals.passed} passed, ${totals.failed} failed across ${totals.suites} suites** | Audit-specific runner; mocks do not prove database concurrency or external success. |\n| Structural gates | 8 passed, 2 blocked | Text/structure evidence only. |\n| Current-source probes | 7 accounting behaviors and 3 offline conditions reproduced | Synthetic doubles/storage, no live financial mutation. |\n| Local aggregate data | Stock and sampled arithmetic/tenant checks passed | AP/payroll/reconciliation samples are all DRAFT; final lifecycle denominator is zero. |\n| TypeScript and browser | Typecheck timed out; app URL refused connection | No current full build/typecheck, authenticated browser or accessibility certification. |\n\nA zero mismatch on draft fixtures is not a finalized workflow pass. Similarly, no evidence of an exploit is not a security certificate. The audit explicitly separates implementation defects, intentional capability restrictions, scanner/test drift and missing release evidence.\n\n## Business decision and next work\n\nFirst stabilize offline facts and financial meaning: F01–F04 and F07. Make source-owner correction and close contracts consistent next, including fiscal/calendar and report corrections. Then exercise all final lifecycles in a disposable PostgreSQL/browser environment and qualify the explicitly supported country/provider versions. Build additional BI only on those corrected sources.\n\n${report('remediation-roadmap.md','Dependency-ordered remediation roadmap')} gives owners, acceptance evidence and migration implications. ${report('findings.md','Detailed findings')} contains concrete repro scenarios and source links. ${report('bi-capability-matrix.md','BI capability matrix')} separates essential completions from later additions.\n\n## Deliverables\n\n- ${report('findings.md')} and ${report('verification-summary.md')}.\n- ${report('schema-assessment.md')}, ${report('schema-model-inventory.csv')} and ${report('model-ownership-register.csv')}.\n- ${report('capability-coverage.csv')} and ${report('review-lens-register.md')}.\n- ${report('architecture-and-workflows.md')} and ${report('bi-capability-matrix.md')}.\n- ${report('remediation-roadmap.md')} and ${report('evidence-index.md')}.\n\nThis audit does not grant legal, tax, accounting, privacy, security, accessibility or production-release certification.`);

write('architecture-and-workflows.md',`# Architecture and end-to-end workflow assessment\n\n## Architecture observed\n\nStoquify is a modular Next.js application with server actions/API boundaries, domain services, Prisma/PostgreSQL persistence, event/outbox processing, evidence/assurance services and separate operational/financial read models. The correct unit of verification is the complete source-owned business transition, including its corrective path. A model, route or a successful journal insert alone is insufficient.\n\n\`\`\`mermaid\nflowchart LR\n U[Role and locale aware UI] --> A[Protected action or API]\n A --> G[Tenant permission entitlement and fresh auth]\n G --> S[Owning domain service]\n S --> T[Transaction: source facts and effects]\n T --> D[(PostgreSQL)]\n T --> E[Business event and outbox]\n E --> W[Workers and external adapters]\n D --> R[Read models and reports]\n D --> Q[Assurance and source evidence]\n Q --> R\n R --> U\n W --> P[Provider or authority evidence]\n P --> S\n\`\`\`\n\nGraph navigation was used as requested: graph_actions contains node analytics_financial_analytics_getfinancialmetrics in community 70 and analytics_financial_reports_getfinancialsummaryreport in community 60. The loaded actions graph has 1,044 nodes/1,284 edges, differing from older AGENTS.md counts. Graphs reflect an older snapshot; current source, not those communities, established the relationships below. ${report('verification/graph-navigation.json')} preserves samples/counts for all five graph files.\n\n## 1. Online sale → money/stock → accounting/reporting\n\nAuthority enters through POS actions and the current cashier/terminal/location/session. ${L('services/pos/pos.service.ts','export async function commitPOSSale')} derives a request hash and first looks up organization+terminal+clientCommitId, validating same-payload replay. It rechecks tender write access, module new-work admission, checkout lease where present, draft sale ownership and scope. Stock/COGS, provider-backed tender or customer receivable, journal posting and business event/outbox are connected in the owning transaction; stored commit results distinguish committed versus completed recovery.\n\n\`\`\`mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n actor Cashier\n participant Action as Protected POS action\n participant POS as POS commit service\n participant DB as Transaction and source facts\n participant Worker as Completion / provider processing\n participant Report as Reports and evidence\n Cashier->>Action: Commit cart with stable command ID\n Action->>POS: Server-derived tenant/user/scope\n POS->>DB: Check prior command, authority and current cart\n POS->>DB: Stock/COGS, tender or AR, journal, event/outbox\n DB-->>POS: Stored commit/recovery result\n POS-->>Cashier: Committed/completed or actionable recovery\n Worker->>DB: Append confirmed provider/settlement evidence\n DB->>Report: Source facts and provenance\n\`\`\`\n\nMoney, fulfillment and sale status are separate facts. Electronic tender must have authoritative provider evidence, not just client success; see ${L('services/payments/pos-electronic-tender-authority.service.ts')}. Store-credit tender explicitly remains unavailable until its authoritative instrument ledger exists. Receipt/fiscal delivery support is also explicitly constrained. Split/partial tender and credit paths must preserve one final obligation, not double-count receipt and receivable.\n\n**Verified:** current service/denial/idempotency unit cohort; local posted POS sale/payment batches. **Open:** actual PostgreSQL competing cashier execution, browser commit ambiguity and real-provider completion. **Known fault:** F02 allows a separate generic journal reversal to break source consistency; F01 affects base-currency reporting.\n\n## 2. Offline sale → local durability → replay/conflict\n\n${L('hooks/posHooks/useOfflineSync.ts')} calls the local queue and server replay/review boundary. The queue retains hashes/sequences and exposes pending/conflict state, but F03/F04 demonstrate that its persistence/restart algorithm is not robust. ${L('services/pos/offline-sync.service.ts','function isOfflinePOSReplayAuthorized')} deliberately denies replay outside the test-authorized pilot.\n\n\`\`\`mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n actor Cashier\n participant Queue as Local queue\n participant Hook as Sync hook\n participant Replay as Replay service\n participant POS as POS source owner\n Cashier->>Queue: Persist provisional sale and stable identity\n Note over Queue: Concurrent enqueue/corruption defect F03\n Hook->>Queue: Mark SYNCING\n Note over Queue,Hook: Restart/throw can strand item F04\n Hook->>Replay: Event envelope, device, sequence, payload hash\n Replay->>Replay: Authorization and pilot admission\n Note over Replay: Production replay currently disabled F05\n Replay->>POS: Idempotent source command if admitted\n POS-->>Replay: Prior or new committed result\n Replay-->>Hook: Applied, rejected or conflict evidence\n Hook->>Queue: Persist acknowledgement or recovery state\n\`\`\`\n\nDevice revocation, stale permissions, altered payloads, duplicate/reordered delivery and resolution authorization must be verified against server truth. A queue hash is an integrity aid, not encryption or an authorization grant. Offline local data protection, tenant/session handover, browser eviction/quota, clock skew and multi-device stock conflicts remain unproven. No offline HRIS, payroll, AP, bank settlement or professional-close workflow was established; do not imply that offline POS scaffolding covers those domains.\n\n## 3. Purchase order → receipt → AP → payment\n\nPurchase-order services own order/receipt transitions; ${L('services/purchasing/ap-control.service.ts')} and specialized preparation/approval/release/recovery services own AP. The schema distinguishes invoice matching and match exceptions, payment request/approval/release, allocation, and posting evidence. Supplier/PO locks, duplicate fingerprints, source revisions and maker-checker support controlled workflows.\n\n\`\`\`mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n actor Buyer\n participant PO as Order/receipt owner\n participant AP as AP matching and approval\n participant DB as Source transaction and evidence\n participant Pay as Payment release/recovery\n participant GL as Accounting/reconciliation\n Buyer->>PO: Approve order and receive partial quantity\n PO->>DB: Receipt and stock facts\n Buyer->>AP: Capture invoice with original source evidence\n AP->>DB: Duplicate check, match/tolerance, exception decision\n AP->>DB: Approved invoice and posting/recovery identity\n Pay->>DB: Separate approver, destination and allocation checks\n Pay->>GL: Release/payment evidence and posting\n GL-->>DB: Settlement/reconciliation status and evidence\n\`\`\`\n\n**Verified:** unit suite, structural gate and consistent tenant/supplier/currency across 24 local allocations. **Limit:** all sampled invoices/payments are DRAFT; final AP→GL and settlement tie-outs have no live denominator. A provider release, a payment record and a settled supplier balance must remain distinct. Landed-cost allocation, full lot/serial/expiry and conversion workflows were not demonstrated merely because supporting fields exist.\n\n## 4. Return/refund/credit → compensating facts\n\n${L('services/purchasing/purchase-return-credit.service.ts')} and ${L('services/purchasing/purchase-correction-ledger.service.ts')} separate purchase-return/credit facts from original receipt/order facts. POS return/refund/void paths have source-owned stock and payment handling. Accounting customer settlement/suspense already receive explicit generic-reversal protection; other sources do not (F02).\n\n\`\`\`mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n actor Operator\n participant Owner as Original domain owner\n participant Stock as Stock disposition\n participant Money as Obligation / tender correction\n participant Ledger as Linked compensating journal\n Operator->>Owner: Authorized correction with original reference\n Owner->>Owner: Validate remaining refundable/returnable quantity\n Owner->>Stock: Return, quarantine, loss or no-stock disposition\n Owner->>Money: Correct allocation/balance or request rail refund\n Owner->>Ledger: Source-linked compensating entry\n Ledger-->>Owner: Retained correction evidence\n Note over Owner,Ledger: Generic journal reversal must not bypass source owner\n\`\`\`\n\nDuplicate corrections, partial returns, refund-before-provider-confirmation, closed periods and mixed return dispositions need isolated end-to-end proof. The desired cross-domain correction contract is explicit above; not every arrow has been executed against a real provider/database during this audit.\n\n## 5. Employee onboarding → payroll → payment/declaration\n\nHRIS owns employment source truth while existing PayrollEmployee/PayrollContract storage is retained for compatibility. Effective organization/manager scope, compensation approval, document retention/legal-hold/scan evidence, attendance/leave and input readiness precede frozen payroll snapshots. ${L('services/payroll/payroll-control.service.ts','export async function calculatePayrollRun')} implements calculation, review, approval, payslip emission and posting as separate transitions with evidence/version checks.\n\n\`\`\`mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n actor HR\n participant H as HRIS source owner\n participant P as Payroll engine\n participant A as Independent reviewer/approver\n participant DB as Immutable evidence\n participant External as Payment and declaration adapters\n HR->>H: Employee, contract, approved compensation, time inputs\n H->>P: Effective and ready source snapshot\n P->>DB: Versioned country rules and calculation snapshot\n P->>A: Review then approval with fresh authority\n A->>DB: CAS transition and event evidence\n P->>DB: Payslip emission and journal posting\n P->>External: Authorized payment/declaration lifecycle\n External-->>DB: Settlement/acceptance or recoverable failure\n\`\`\`\n\nService and migration evidence supports historical reproducibility better than live recalculation from mutable employee records. Actual final-cycle proof is absent in the local sample: every run is DRAFT. Correct arithmetic on those rows does not establish statutory eligibility, emitted payroll, settlement or declared obligations. Qualified country/version approval is a separate gate (F11). The manager fallback is current-only; do not treat it as historical reporting/delegation evidence. Employee detail must remain redacted from general BI/support output.\n\n## 6. Period close → assurance → reporting → later correction\n\n${L('services/accounting/periods.service.ts','export async function closeAccountingPeriod')} takes an exclusive period row lock. Transactional posting obtains a shared lock through getOpenPeriodForDate. That improves ordering versus earlier audits. However, the current close preflight is narrower than the complete assurance model (F06).\n\n\`\`\`mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n actor Accountant\n participant Close as Period-close service\n participant DB as Period and source facts\n participant Assurance as Close assurance / certified export\n participant Correction as Source correction owner\n Accountant->>Close: Close with permission and fresh authentication\n Close->>DB: Lock period and run current preflight\n DB-->>Close: Draft/batch/payment/balance checks\n Close->>DB: Set CLOSED if checks pass\n Note over Close,Assurance: Wider assurance not consumed here (F06)\n Accountant->>Assurance: Request qualified evidence/export\n Assurance->>DB: Evaluate source versions, blockers, grants and proofs\n Correction->>DB: Controlled later correction, original evidence retained\n DB-->>Assurance: Invalidate/re-evaluate affected evidence\n\`\`\`\n\nA CLOSED period, balanced trial balance, signed reconciliation and certified export are distinct states. Source completeness must include stock, AP/AR, payroll, taxes, clearing and all admitted producers. Later changes must invalidate the right versioned close/export evidence, not rewrite history.\n\n## Cross-cutting workflow and UX observations\n\nRoute families exist for accounting, payroll, HRIS, POS, purchases, analytics, reconciliation and manager/accountant workspaces. App and component code provides role/module-denied, loading, error, stale, partial and recovery states. BI contracts also represent redacted and unavailable values. Supplementary action/component tests passed; these are not live usability/accessibility evidence. The attempted local browser entry point was unavailable.\n\nMaterial support/UX questions for the next runtime audit: Can a cashier distinguish committed-but-not-completed from rejected? Can AP resume a failed posting without duplicate payment? Can the accountant see lock-only versus evidence-complete close? Can employees use EN/FR self-service on mobile with keyboard/screen reader? Can support diagnose failed delivery/worker leases without seeing protected payroll content?\n\nPackaging should expose those supported boundaries. Module admission uses shared locks and a stop-new-work policy that preserves permitted completion; billing/grants must not strand existing financial obligations. ${L('services/modules/module-admission.service.ts')}. Agent tool policy is read-only and permission-scoped in the inspected path: ${L('services/agents/agent-policy.service.ts')}. Worker recovery exists in ${L('services/events/business-event-outbox.service.ts')}, but deployment schedules, alerts, saturation and retry latency were not observed.\n\n## Remaining verification matrix\n\n| Scenario | Required proof | Current status |\n|---|---|---|\n| Two cashiers buy last unit | Disposable PostgreSQL concurrent commands; one stock winner, no orphan money/journal | Unit/static design only |\n| Duplicate sale/provider callback and changed payload | One source effect; changed payload rejected; callback attribution retained | Unit evidence; provider sandbox absent |\n| Offline restart, corruption, multiple tabs, revoked device | Durable record conservation and authoritative reconciliation | Reproduced local failures; full browser/device test outstanding |\n| Partial receipt/invoice/payment and correction | Stock, AP, allocation and journal tie out after each transition | Unit evidence; no finalized local AP sample |\n| Payroll source changes after snapshot; approval races | Historical output stable; one transition winner; no partial event/outbox | Unit/trigger evidence; actual concurrency not run |\n| Close versus source commit/correction | Consistent cutoff and stale evidence invalidation | Lock design inspected; full multi-producer DB race unverified |\n| Unauthorized tenant/role/delegated/expired-grant access | Request-level denial and no private data leakage | Selected service/action tests; complete adversarial matrix absent |\n| Current fresh migration/upgrade/restore | 87 migrations plus functional identity/source/ledger recovery | Metadata parity only; older 80-migration restore evidence |\n| Report large data, pagination, currencies, late corrections | Independent totals, stable cutoffs and bounded latency | Currency defects reproduced; load/pagination system proof absent |`);

